# <type>: <description>
# 
# [optional body]
# 
# [optional footer(s)]

# Type 
#
# The type must be non-empty, and all lower case. The following is a list of accepted types.
#
# 1. chore: For commits that complete routine/automated tasks such as upgrading dependencies.
#
# 2. deprecate: For commits that deprecate functionality.
#
# 3. feat: For commits that add new functionality to ours.
#
# 4. fix: For commits that fix bugs/errors in ours.
#
# 5. release: For commits that relate to the release of a new version.
#
# 6. refactor: For commits that improve existing code without adding new functionality or fixing bugs.
#
# 7. style: For commits that improve the formatting/style of the code.
#
# 8. test: For commits that add tests or improve test coverage.
#
# 9. docs: For commits that improve the documentation.
#
# 10. ci: For commits that improve the continuous integration (CI) pipeline.
#
# 11. perf: For commits that improve the performance of the code.
#
# 12. build: For commits that change the build system: GN files, build arguments, toolchain set-up.

# Description
#
# The description must be non-empty, and must be under 256 characters.

# Body
# The body is optional. If it is provided there should be a blank line between it and the description. 
# It must be broken into lines of no more than 256 characters.
# 
# Note that usually, it is advisable to put this kind of information in your pull request description, 
# in addition to/rather than directly in the commit.

# Footer
# The footer is optional. If it is provided there should be a blank line between it and the body. 
# It must be broken into lines of no more than 256 characters.
# 
# The footer must start with a keyword (e.g. Closes, Resolves, Related to etc.) and optionally a ticket number.
#
# Examples:
#
# Resolves: #123
# Related to: #123, #456
# See also: #789